WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.520 align:start position:19%
Sunday night, November 22nd,
1987,

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.520 align:start position:19%
Chicago, Illinois.
9:00 p.m.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:15.680 align:start position:19%
and the evening news is on.
The sports highlights are wrapping up.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:20.760 align:start position:19%
Then,
the screen goes black.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:24.200 align:start position:19%
For about 25 seconds, something else
fills the screen, something that was

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.480 align:start position:19%
never supposed to be there.
A figure in a rubber mask rocking side

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:32.520 align:start position:19%
to side humming,
silent, staring straight at you.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.600 align:start position:19%
Then the news snaps back and the station
acts like it never happened.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.440 align:start position:19%
Honestly, this part still gives me
chills because 2 hours later,

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:45.720 align:start position:19%
he came back.
And this time, he stayed for 90 seconds.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:49.760 align:start position:19%
To understand what happened that night,
you need to know who Max Headroom was.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.920 align:start position:19%
Max Headroom was the first
computer-generated TV personality, a

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:58.200 align:start position:19%
wise-cracking, stuttering host from the
mid-80s.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.640 align:start position:19%
He had his own show. He starred in soda
commercials. Everybody in America knew

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:07.600 align:start position:19%
that face.
And that rubber mask, you could buy one

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:12.360 align:start position:19%
at any costume shop for a few dollars.
But on one Sunday night in Chicago,

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:16.360 align:start position:19%
somebody turned that joke into a federal
crime.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:22.040 align:start position:19%
And nearly four decades later,
nobody has ever been caught.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:25.920 align:start position:19%
Let's go back to 9:14 p.m. WGN, Channel
9, Chicago's biggest independent

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:29.360 align:start position:19%
station.
The sports anchor is on screen, all

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:33.520 align:start position:19%
smiles, running through the football
highlights.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:38.000 align:start position:19%
Then the picture freezes.
The audio holds,

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:40.880 align:start position:19%
and the screen cuts to black.
For 25 seconds, the masked figure is

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:47.120 align:start position:19%
there,
rocking, humming, saying nothing at all.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:51.240 align:start position:19%
Inside the WGN control room, engineers
watch in horror as their own signal gets

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:55.440 align:start position:19%
stopped.
But WGN has a trick ready,

00:01:53.600 --> 00:02:00.640 align:start position:19%
a backup transmitter link.
They switch over in seconds.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:05.479 align:start position:19%
Just like that, the news is back. 25
seconds, no audio, gone.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:08.240 align:start position:19%
Most viewers thought their TV was
broken,

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:12.120 align:start position:19%
but the intruder wasn't done. He was
just warming up.

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:15.720 align:start position:19%
Because he had found a second target, a
station with no backup at all.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:22.360 align:start position:19%
11:15 p.m.
WTTW

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:28.600 align:start position:19%
Channel 11
Chicago's public television station.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:33.600 align:start position:19%
Doctor Who was on, a calm nerdy Sunday
night ritual for thousands of viewers.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:39.160 align:start position:19%
And then the screen distorts.
The doctor vanishes mid-sentence.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:44.640 align:start position:19%
A figure steps into frame against a
sheet of rippling corrugated metal.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:48.200 align:start position:19%
The mask, the sunglasses, a beige suit
jacket,

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:51.120 align:start position:19%
a red tie.
Behind him,

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:56.080 align:start position:19%
a second person,
barely visible,

00:02:52.800 --> 00:03:01.720 align:start position:19%
waves the metal sheet up and down.
The figure sways, and then he starts

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:05.560 align:start position:19%
humming the theme to an old cartoon.
The audio kicks in,

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:10.400 align:start position:19%
buzzing, warped, like a voice bubbling
up from underwater.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:15.080 align:start position:19%
"That does it," the figure rasps.
"He's a freaking nerd."

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:18.800 align:start position:19%
He holds up a can of Pepsi and sneers
the old slogan,

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:21.640 align:start position:19%
"Catch the wave."
"My brother is wearing the other one,"

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:26.320 align:start position:19%
he says,
tapping the mask on his own face.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:30.480 align:start position:19%
He rips off a rubber glove and hurls it
straight at the camera.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:35.600 align:start position:19%
Then he turns around
and bends over right into the lens.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:39.560 align:start position:19%
His accomplice spanks him with a fly
swatter. He howls, "They're coming to

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:43.519 align:start position:19%
get me!"
And then almost calmly, "I just made a

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:48.440 align:start position:19%
giant masterpiece for all the greatest
world newspaper nerds."

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:52.360 align:start position:19%
That line was a jab at WGN, whose call
letters stand for the world's greatest

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:57.000 align:start position:19%
newspaper.
He hums again. He makes an obscene

00:03:54.760 --> 00:04:03.440 align:start position:19%
gesture at the camera on public
television at 11:00 at night.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:07.640 align:start position:19%
90 seconds and then the signal dies. The
doctor's back mid-sentence like nothing

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:12.320 align:start position:19%
happened.
No warning, no explanation, just static

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:15.040 align:start position:19%
and a city full of people asking, "Did
you see that?"

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:22.160 align:start position:19%
In my opinion, the scariest part isn't
the mask. It's that someone rehearsed

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:26.520 align:start position:19%
all of this down to the second and
nobody stopped them.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:31.040 align:start position:19%
Within minutes, the phone lines at both
stations were melting down.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:35.640 align:start position:19%
Hundreds of callers.
Nobody can believe what they just saw.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:38.600 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; I'm Ali Noble. Welcome to True Crime
Files.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:43.040 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Quick one. If you want a new unsolved
case every single day, hit subscribe. It

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:46.000 align:start position:19%
really keeps this channel going.
By morning, it's the biggest story in

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:50.120 align:start position:19%
Chicago.
And by the end of the week,

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:54.040 align:start position:19%
the whole country.
So, how do you hijack a TV station in

00:04:51.919 --> 00:04:59.600 align:start position:19%
1987?
Honestly, the method was almost elegant.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:04.080 align:start position:19%
Every station beams its picture from the
studio to its transmitter tower by

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:09.919 align:start position:19%
microwave link.
The hijacker aimed his own more powerful

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:13.280 align:start position:19%
transmitter at that same tower.
When his signal overpowered the

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:18.360 align:start position:19%
station's, his video won.
Engineers call it an STL override.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:22.840 align:start position:19%
But, here's the catch. Microwaves need a
clear line of sight. He had to be up

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:27.720 align:start position:19%
high close by.
That means expensive gear, real

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:31.000 align:start position:19%
know-how, and nerves of steel.
This was no kid with a remote control.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:35.480 align:start position:19%
This was someone who understood
broadcast engineering.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:41.120 align:start position:19%
WGN survived because its engineers
flipped to a backup link in seconds.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:46.200 align:start position:19%
WTTW had no backup. For the full 90
seconds, they were completely helpless.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:51.840 align:start position:19%
And the audio?
Experts noticed it was badly distorted,

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:55.480 align:start position:19%
like it was recorded at home first.
The theory, he taped the whole

00:05:53.280 --> 00:06:00.200 align:start position:19%
performance on a VCR, then played it
into the transmitter live.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:04.880 align:start position:19%
The mask, the metal sheet, the soda can,
the fly swatter, a costume, a set, a

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:11.960 align:start position:19%
script.
Somebody built a tiny pirate TV studio

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:16.240 align:start position:19%
in 1987 and aimed it at an entire city.
Which raises the real question,

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:20.720 align:start position:19%
if he planned it this carefully,
why?

00:06:17.919 --> 00:06:25.040 align:start position:19%
Was it a prank, a protest, a message
nobody was meant to understand?

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:29.040 align:start position:19%
The authorities wanted answers, too, and
they came fast.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:33.760 align:start position:19%
The FCC launched an investigation within
days. Hijacking a broadcast signal is a

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:39.360 align:start position:19%
federal crime.
We're talking massive fines and years in

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:44.160 align:start position:19%
federal prison.
The FBI joined in. Agents interviewed

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:48.800 align:start position:19%
every engineer at both stations.
They mapped every rooftop and hilltop

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:51.600 align:start position:19%
with a line of sight to the towers.
But a microwave burst leaves no

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:55.160 align:start position:19%
fingerprints.
The trail went cold

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:58.919 align:start position:19%
fast.
The theories started coming.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:02.840 align:start position:19%
A disgruntled ex-employee settling a
score?

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:08.000 align:start position:19%
A college engineering prank that went
way too far?

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:12.880 align:start position:19%
A performance artist chasing the biggest
audience imaginable?

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:16.520 align:start position:19%
One theory says he knew WGN's backup
routine, which smells like an inside

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:19.840 align:start position:19%
job.
Another says the whole thing was a test,

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:23.520 align:start position:19%
a dry run for something bigger that
never came.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:28.200 align:start position:19%
The statute of limitations ran out in
the early '90s. Even if they found him

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:31.240 align:start position:19%
today, no charges.
And I'll be honest, I think the mask is

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:36.280 align:start position:19%
still sitting in someone's attic in
Chicago, waiting.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:40.240 align:start position:19%
So, why does this still matter almost 40
years later?

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:45.760 align:start position:19%
Because it was the first great unsolved
hack of the television age.

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:51.000 align:start position:19%
Before hackers, before the internet,
someone hacked the air itself.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:55.440 align:start position:19%
It inspired copycats, small signal
intrusions in the years that followed.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:59.680 align:start position:19%
But nobody ever matched the audacity of
those 90 seconds.

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:03.920 align:start position:19%
Every few years someone claims to know
who it was. An old rumor, a deathbed

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:08.160 align:start position:19%
confession.
None of it has ever checked out. The

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:12.040 align:start position:19%
masked man of Chicago remains a ghost in
the machine.

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:17.240 align:start position:19%
A 90-second ghost that a whole city
watched together, live.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:22.120 align:start position:19%
If you were watching WTTW that night in
1987, I need to hear your story.

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:27.280 align:start position:19%
Tell me in the comments.
And tell me what you think. Prank,

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:31.000 align:start position:19%
protest, or something stranger?
